abstract: >
  EAR is a system software for energy management, accounting
  and optimization for super computers. This features are
  offered by EAR application manager together with a
  scheduler plugin. The scheduler plugin (SPANK plugin in
  the case of SLURM) offers a transparent and fully
  integrated solution into the scheduler so the user doesn't
  have to specify any submission flag, hint or application
  modification. EAR supports three scopes of accounting:
  first, at job level, so system reports can be generated
  with exhaustive information about energy consumption per
  jobs, users, groups, accounts etc. Second, at application
  process scope, a detailed and powerful performance and
  power monitoring that can be used later for detailed
  accounting, application analysis and static optimization
  and finally at runtime (per-process), where runtime
  signatures measured to compute the process signatures and
  to apply the energy optimization are also reported.

  Node and system monitoring are also provided by the EAR
  services: EAR node manager (EARD), EAR DB manager (EARDBD)
  and EAR System power manager (EARGM). 

  The EARDBD is a distributed service offering buffering and
  aggregation of data, minimising the number of connections
  with the DB server. It can be considered as part of the
  monitoring services because it generates aggregated energy
  records.

  EAR supports a powerful and flexible configuration where
  different architectures with different power limits can be
  configured in the same cluster. CPU and CPU+GPU nodes are
  supported.
